X:1
T:Ffarwel Fo i Langyfelach Lon
T:Farewell to Gay Llangyfelach
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=8 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by Bertie Stephens (born 1900), a breeder of hunting dogs, of Llwyncelyn, Llangeitho, Ceredigion; recorded 25.1.62 (St Fagans tape 458). He was brought up in Abergorlech, Carmarthenshire, knew dozens of folk songs by heart and sang many of them in concerts. He learnt this one from his father, a Tregaron man. He sang only verses 1 to 6 (the soldier's farewell letter) at this recording, and nine verses four years later; the full text here is from "Caneuon Serch, Hen a Diweddar". The ballad was once popular all over Wales (in the north, with Caernarfon instead of Llangyfelach, which is near Swansea). Verses 1 to 6 are the farewell of a Welshman newly enlisted in the Light Dragoons, about to sail with the English fleet; in the rest his sweetheart waits for him, and he comes home and marries her. The words are by James Turberville of Glamorgan, perhaps the itinerant weaver of that name who lived in the parish of Llangynwyd. Compare the tune of "Lisa Lân".
N:The singer's main differences from the printed words: 1.3 mynd gal gweld pa; 3.3 dryms a'r ffidls yn cadw sŵn; 4.1 'Rôl imi gyrraedd Llunden; 4.2 Daeth diwti galed; 5.1 A llythyr ddaeth; 5.4 I frwydro dros y cefnfor glas; 6.3 Mewn sense ac oedran o flaen y tân; 7.1 Os hola rh(y)wun; 7.2 Dywedwch wrtho; 7.3 'Rhon a weddïe; 11.1 Wel, cymrwch gyngor; 11.2 Os â; 11.3 O, peidiwch; 11.4 nes daw e'n ôl.
N:The second A in bars 2 and 4 and the F on "Ai" in bar 6 are bracketed on the score: their length varied between a crotchet and a quaver from verse to verse. The bracketed D on "i" in bar 3 and "pa" in bar 5 was not needed for the words of verse 1 as recorded. The slur in bar 7 is dotted on the score.
